{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Foam on the Wrack Line: Zostera Seagrass Wasting Disease",
    "date": "2026-09-25",
    "lesson": "In a temperate eelgrass (Zostera marina) meadow, the protist Labyrinthula zosterae chronically damages seagrass leaf tissue, opening wounds that leaking plant sugars and amino acids turn into hotspots for opportunistic heterotrophic bacteria. As leaves die and shred, that plant detritus becomes a slow-release carbohydrate-rich food source, while wound fluids skew locally protein-and-lipid-rich. The lesson: seagrass die-off is not just a plant disease, it restructures the whole microbial community around it, shifting the food web from live photosynthate to a decomposer-driven detrital economy, and grazers cluster wherever the wounds keep leaking.",
    "citation": "Short & Mathieson (1988); Muehlstein et al. (1991) on Labyrinthula zosterae wasting disease",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Zostera marina seagrass wasting disease, temperate NW Atlantic coastal meadow"
  },
